      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this certificate carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML-CS Certificate issued under Scheme A by METAS (Switzerland), Number R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04 Rev. 3.\n\nKey data:\n- certificate.number: \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\" with revision 3. The full number shown is \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04 Rev. 3\". I'll use \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\" as number and maybe note revision. The schema doesn't have revision in certificate, but revision_history exists. I'll put number as \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\".\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- member_state: \"Switzerland\"\n- date_issued: \"2021-04-15\" (3003 Bern-Wabern, 15 April 2021)\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"CH1\" (The OIML Issuing Authority CH1)\n- page_total: 4 (pages 1/4 to 4/4)\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Federal Institute of Metrology METAS\"\n- address_lines: [\"Lindenweg 50\", \"3003 Bern-Wabern\", \"Switzerland\"] — the address is \"Lindenweg 50, 3003 Bern-Wabern, Switzerland\". I could split into lines.\n- person_responsible: \"Gulian Couvreur\"\n- person_title: \"Head of METAS-Cert\" (from issuing authority table). Later \"Head of sector METAS-Cert\". I'll use \"Head of METAS-Cert\".\n- phone: \"+41 58 387 01 11\"\n- website: \"www.metas.ch\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"CH1\"\n\napplicants:\n- name: \"Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG\"\n- address_lines: [\"Otto-Brenner-Strasse 20\", \"37079 Göttingen\", \"Germany\"]\n\nmanufacturers:\n- name: \"Sartorius Scientific Instruments (Beijing) Co, Limited\"\n- address_lines: [\"Yu An Road No. 33, Zone B\", \"Tianzhu Airport Industrialzone, Shunyi District\", \"101 300 Beijing\", \"PR China\"]\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" — well, R76 is for non-automatic weighing instruments. The schema example categories include \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\"... For R76 balances, the category would be \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" or \"Balance\". The type is \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n- type_designations: [\"BC-EA\", \"BC-EB\", \"BC-EC\", \"BC-ED\", \"BC-EE\", \"BC-EF\", \"BC-EG\", \"BC-EH\", \"BC-AA\", \"BC-AB\", \"BC-AC\", \"BC-AD\", \"BC-AE\", \"BC-AF\", \"BC-AG\", \"BC-AH\"]. The type field says \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\" and Module: \"BC-EA to BC-EH, BC-AA to BC-AH\". The tables list pairs: BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB, BC-EC/BC-AC, BC-ED/BC-AD, BC-EE/BC-AE, BC-EF/BC-AF, BC-EG/BC-AG, BC-EH/BC-AH. So 16 models.\n- module_designation: The \"Module\" field here is actually model range: \"BC-EA to BC-EH, BC-AA to BC-AH\". Hmm, in this certificate, \"Module\" means the model variants covered, not OIML module (like \"Analog load cell\"). So for module_designation, which per schema is \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null — this is a balance, not a module system. I'd say null or \"Not applicable\". Since the certificate's \"Module\" row actually lists the models, I shouldn't put that in module_designation. I'll set module_designation to null and capture the model range in type_designations or model_family.\n- description: \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"BC-E and BC-A Line\" or \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". I'll use \"BC-E and BC-A Line\".\n- models: 16 models with attributes.\n\nModels and their characteristics (from the four tables):\n\nTable 1 (page 2):\nColumn 1: BC-EA / BC-AA, Class I, Max 50 g - 320 g, e 1 mg - 2 mg, d 0.1 mg - 2 mg, n ≤ 320 000, Tare-balancing 100% of Max, Temp range 1: +17/+27 °C, Temp range 2 (1): +10/+30 °C, Nominal capacity load receptor: 384 g, Initial zero setting + dead load (2): ≤ 234 g, Max pan size: Ø 90 mm.\n\nWait, but note the Max for class I: \"50 g - 320 g\" means Max ranges from 50 g to 320 g within this model group (i.e., different capacity variants?). Actually for balances, Max can vary; the certificate gives a range of possible Max values. Hmm. Actually for BC-EA/BC-AA: Max ∈ [50 g, 320 g], e ∈ [1 mg, 2 mg], d ∈ [0.1 mg, 2 mg], n ≤ 320 000.\n\nColumn 2: BC-EB / BC-AB, Class I, Max 50 g - 220 g, e 1 mg - 2 mg, d 0.1 mg - 2 mg, n ≤ 220 000, Temp range 1: +17/+27, Temp range 2: +10/+30, Nominal capacity: 264 g, Initial zero + dead load ≤ 214 g, pan Ø 90 mm.\n\nColumn 3: BC-EB / BC-AB again (likely OCR typo; should be BC-EC/BC-AC? No wait — the third column header says \"BC-EB BC-AB\" with Class (II), Max 0.1 g - 100 g. Hmm, that's odd. Looking at table 2, BC-EC/BC-AC is Class I Max 500–1500 g. So the third column of table 1 should be... Actually the pattern: each table row \"Type\" lists two models per column (E and A variants of the same letter). Table 1 columns: (BC-EA, BC-AA), (BC-EB, BC-AB), (BC-EB, BC-AB)? That can't be right. Since BC-EA to BC-EH covers A–H: EA, EB, EC, ED, EE, EF, EG, EH. Table 1 has 3 columns: EA/AA, EB/AB, and the third should be EC/AC? But table 2 has EC/AC and ED/AD. Hmm.\n\nWait: The models are EA through EH (8 letters). Table 1: EA/AA, EB/AB, EB/AB(?). Table 2: EC/AC, ED/AD. Table 3 (page 3): EE/AE, EF/AF. Table 4: EG/AG, EH/AH.\n\nSo the third column in Table 1 is likely a typo in OCR or in the original — it should be a distinct model. Given the progression, table 1 col 3 with class II, Max 0.1-100 g... Hmm, but BC-EC in table 2 is Class I 500-1500 g. So the third column of table 1 can't be EC.\n\nActually, maybe the third column of table 1 IS \"BC-EB BC-AB\" again but as class II variant? That seems odd. Alternatively the original document might have \"BC-EC BC-AC\" in table 1 col 3 as Class II with Max 0.1-100 g, and table 2's first column \"BC-EC BC-AC\" as Class I 500-1500 g? That would conflict.\n\nHmm, let me think about what the actual Sartorius BC-E/BC-A line looks like. Sartorius Bal BC-E and BC-A... These are actually \"Entris\" or similar? BC-E... Actually these are likely Sartorius \"BCA\" / \"BCE\" balances (like BCA2201S etc.). The letter after BC- indicates capacity/size: A through H.\n\nGiven the certificate text says models BC-EA to BC-EH and BC-AA to BC-AH (8+8 = 16 models), each table column pairs E and A variant. Table 1 has 3 columns but the third repeats EB/AB. This might be an OCR error for something like \"BC-E? BC-A?\"... Given tables 3 and 4 each have 2 columns covering EE-EH, and table 2 has 2 columns covering EC-ED, then table 1's 3 columns would cover EA, EB, and... only EA, EB needed? No — that leaves 16 - 4 - 4 - 4 = 4 for table 1's 3 columns which would be 6 models. Contradiction.\n\nHmm wait, let me recount: 16 models total. Table 2: EC/AC, ED/AD → 4 models. Table 3: EE/AE, EF/AF → 4 models. Table 4: EG/AG, EH/AH → 4 models. That's 12. Table 1 needs to cover EA/AA and EB/AB → 4 models, but it has 3 columns. So the third column is likely a duplicate/error, OR table 1 col 3 is a different designation not following the letter pattern.\n\nActually, maybe the original document has three columns in table 1: BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB, and BC-EB/BC-AB again — as two variants of EB (class I and class II)? The second EB column has Class (II) with Max 0.1-100 g, n ≤ 100 000. Hmm, that's possible: the same model family BC-EB/BC-AB exists in class II with different Max range.\n\nActually wait — look at temperature range: col 3 has Temp range 1 = +10/+30 °C (unlike col 1 and 2 which have +17/+27). And e = 1 mg - 2 mg but d = 0.1 mg - 2 mg with n ≤ 100 000, Max 0.1 g - 100 g. For class II with e = 0.1 g? No wait, class II requires e ≥ 0.1 g? Actually class II (high accuracy) requires e ≥ 0.01 g typically (e between 0.01 g and 50 kg). Hmm, class (II) with e = 1-2 mg is not allowed under R76 (class II minimum e is 0.1 g? Actually I think class II: e ≥ 0.001 kg? Let me recall: Class I: e ≥ 1 mg (with e = 1 mg having verification exemption); Class II: e ≥ 0.1 g; Class III: e ≥ 1 g; Class IIII: e ≥ 100 g? Hmm, not exactly. R76-1: Class special (I): e ≥ 1 mg; high (II): 0.1 g ≤ e ≤ 50 kg? I believe class II minimum is 0.1 g. So class II with e = 1-2 mg doesn't make sense. So the third column of table 1 probably is class (I) too? But OCR says (II).\n\nHmm, this is a real METAS certificate. Let me think about what it actually is. This is certificate R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04 for Sartorius BC-E and BC-A line balances (these are the \"Bal\" series? Actually BC-E = \"Balance Cubis\"? No...).\n\nActually, these might be the Sartorius \"BCE\" and \"BCA\" balances — e.g., BCA2201S-0CCN... Hmm. Actually the modern Sartorius BC-E / BC-A are the \"Cubis II\"? Not sure.\n\nGiven uncertainty, I should just faithfully represent what the markdown says. The third column header says \"BC-EB BC-AB\" — but that duplicates column 2. Since I can't fabricate, I have choices:\n1. Treat it as given: BC-EB/BC-AB appears twice with different characteristics. That would be weird in a model_family (duplicate model_id).\n2. Note it as a separate model variant.\n\nHmm. Actually, maybe I should reconsider: perhaps the OCR mangled \"BC-EC BC-AC\" (since C and B look similar?) and table 2's first column is different. But table 2 col 1 clearly says \"BC-EC BC-AC\" with class I, Max 500-1500 g. And table 1 col 3 says class II Max 0.1-100 g. If both were EC/AC, we'd have conflicting data for the same model. Unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, maybe table 1 col 3 is \"BC-EB BC-AB\" indeed — a second variant of EB/AB in class II with lower capacity. Some balance families have the same model designation covering multiple accuracy classes and capacity ranges. E.g., \"BCE124I\" vs \"BCE224I\"... Hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the intended models in table 1 col 3 are different: The A-line and E-line... BC-EA to BC-EH, BC-AA to BC-AH. That's exactly 16. Columns: T1: (EA,AA), (EB,AB), (EB?,AB?) — 6 slots for 4 models. Something's off.\n\nPossibility: The original cert table 1 col 3 might be \"BC-EA BC-AA\" again (class II variant)? Or maybe col 3 of the type row got misaligned and actually the real table 1 in the PDF has: BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB, BC-EB/BC-AB where the third is actually \"BC-EB/BC-AB\" printed erroneously in the original too. PDFs sometimes have errors.\n\nGiven rule 8 \"Don't fabricate\", I'll represent what's there. I could create model entries like \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\" for the third column. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the structure. Maybe it's better to treat each table column as a \"variant group\" since each column lists TWO models (E and A variants) that share identical characteristics. The model_family.models can have one entry per model (16 entries), with attributes per column applied to both E and A variants. But the third column duplicates EB/AB...\n\nAlternative: model entries keyed by column: \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", \"BC-EB/BC-AB\", \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\", \"BC-EC/BC-AC\", \"BC-ED/BC-AD\", \"BC-EE/BC-AE\", \"BC-EF/BC-AF\", \"BC-EG/BC-AG\", \"BC-EH/BC-AH\" → 9 column groups.\n\nHmm, but rule says models[] is \"one entry per variant\". The E and A variants share all specs in each column. I think listing 9 groups where each group = {model_id: \"BC-EA / BC-AA\", ...} is reasonable? But then model_id conflates two models.\n\nAlternatively list 18 model entries (each individual model), duplicating the column attributes. And for the duplicate column, name the third one \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\"...\n\nLet me think about which approach best serves the schema's intent. The schema example for R60 uses model_id like \"Emax=25t\" — synthetic IDs when no explicit model names. Here we DO have explicit model names. Each column describes specs common to the E and A variant of a letter. I'll create models for each individual designation, with attributes from the shared column. For the third column of table 1 (duplicate \"BC-EB BC-AB\"), I need to handle: it's class II with different Max. So BC-EB and BC-AB would have TWO spec sets. Hmm.\n\nOption: give model_id \"BC-EB (class II, 0.1–100 g)\" and \"BC-AB (class II, 0.1–100 g)\". That's making up labels though. Alternatively, since the cert says \"BC-EB/BC-AB\" twice, maybe the third column really is a distinct variant and I should include it with a distinguishing label.\n\nActually, you know, let me look at this from the perspective of actual METAS cert. Searching memory: METAS certificate R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04, Sartorius... The BC-E and BC-A line... Actually I recall Sartorius \"BCA\" series are \"Balance Cubis accessories\"? No. There's \"Sartorius BCE\" balances: BCE124I-1CCN, BCE224I-1CCN, BCE324I, BCE5201S... The \"BCE\" = \"Entris II BCE\"? Hmm, Entris II is \"BCE\" indeed! Sartorius Entris II Basic BCE models: BCE124I, BCE224I, BCE324I, BCE423I, BCE5201S, BCE6202S, BCE8201I, BCE124A? No, \"BCE124A\"? Hmm, actually the BC-E line and BC-A line: Entris II BCE and ... \"BCA\"? There might be \"BCE\" (Entris II) and \"BCA\" (Bal?). Hmm whatever — the designations here are BC-EA...BC-EH and BC-AA...BC-AH, so 8 per line.\n\nGiven BC-EA to BC-EH covers letters A-H consecutively, and tables cover: T1: A, B, B(?); T2: C, D; T3: E, F; T4: G, H. Total without duplicate: A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H ✓. So table 1 SHOULD have only 2 columns (A and B). The third column with class II Max 0.1-100 g, n ≤ 100 000... doesn't correspond to any letter.\n\nHold on — maybe table 1's third column IS the second part of the model coverage: the cert might cover balances where the same letter designation appears in both class I and class II? Like \"BC-EA/BC-AA\" exists as class I (Max 50-320g) AND the... no.\n\nAlternatively — maybe the original table 1 col 3 says \"BC-EC BC-AC\" and table 2 col 1 says something else... but OCR of table 2 col 1 header \"BC-EC BC-AC\" is clear. And table 1 col 3 \"BC-EB BC-AB\" is clear. One of them is likely an OCR error from the source PDF. If table 1 col 3 were \"BC-EC BC-AC\" (class II, Max 0.1-100 g, n ≤ 100 000, temp 1: +10/+30), then table 2 col 1 \"BC-EC BC-AC\" (class I, Max 500-1500, n ≤ 150 000, temp 1: +17/+27) would duplicate EC. Same problem symmetric.\n\nHmm hmm. What if the actual letter sequence includes more granularity: maybe it's BC-EA through BC-EH but tables show: T1: EA/AA, EB/AB, EC/AC?; T2: ED/AD, EE/AE?... no, T3 shows EE/AE, EF/AF explicitly and T4 shows EG/AG, EH/AH.\n\nOK here's another thought: maybe table 1 col 3 is \"BC-EB BC-AB\" intentionally — representing the class II version of the same model family. In the original METAS cert (I can try to recall... I don't have it memorized), it's plausible that the BC-EB/BC-AB group covers two classes: class I with Max 50-220 g and class II with Max 0.1-100 g. Wait but class II with e=1-2 mg is metrologically invalid (class II minimum e is 0.1 g per R76? Let me double check: R76-1 2006, class II: 0.1 g ≤ e? Actually I recall the requirement: Class II: e ≥ 0.1 g. Yes, special class I: e ≥ 1 mg (for e=1 mg verification not required below Max 50000e?), class II: e ≥ 0.1 g, class III: e ≥ 1 g, class IIII: e ≥ 0.1 kg? Something like that). So class II with e = 1-2 mg is invalid → the \"Accuracy Class (II)\" for that column is suspicious. But wait, look at the class II columns in table 2: BC-ED/BC-AD, class II, e = 0.01 g – 0.1 g. e = 0.01 g = 10 mg for class II — still below 0.1 g! Hmm, so that violates R76 too? Actually hmm, is the class II minimum e really 0.1 g?\n\nLet me recall R76-1: \"Class (II): 0.1 g ≤ e ≤ 50 kg\"? I'm fairly certain there's a minimum e per class in R76: Class I: e ≥ 1 mg; Class II: e ≥ 0.1 g; Class III: e ≥ 1 g; Class IIII: e ≥ 100 g. Hmm wait, but actually I think it's in the national regulations... In OIML R76-1:2006, Table: Class symbols, verification scale interval: Class special I: e ≥ 1 mg; Class high II: e ≥ 0.1 g? Hmm, actually I believe it's: Class II: e ≥ 0.1 g. But some class II balances have e = 0.01 g... no wait, e = 0.01 g balances are usually class I or class II? A balance with e = 0.01 g, Max = 300 g → n = 30 000. Class II allows n up to 100 000. Hmm, but the e ≥ 0.1 g restriction...\n\nActually, I now recall: R76-1:2006 section 3.2.2 or Table 1: \"Class II: e ≥ 0.1 g\" — hmm, but actually no! Let me think again. I believe the table says: Special class I: e ≥ 1 mg; High class II: e ≥ 0.1 g?? I genuinely recall class II minimum e of 0.1 g being a thing in some regulations. But OIML R76 Table 1 might not restrict minimum e per class; instead: \"Class I: e ≥ 1 mg, Class II: e ≥ 0.1 g\"? Hmm.\n\nRegardless — my job is extraction, not validation. I extract what's written.\n\nSo the duplicate column issue: I'll treat the third column as given: \"BC-EB BC-AB\" — a class II variant. To distinguish in the models list, I'll create entries with distinguishing model_ids. Since each column pairs E and A variants, maybe the cleanest is to have one model entry per column with model_id = \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\" for the third.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — maybe simplest and most faithful: models[] entries:\n- BC-EA / BC-AA\n- BC-EB / BC-AB\n- BC-EB / BC-AB (class II variant)\n- BC-EC / BC-AC\n- BC-ED / BC-AD\n- BC-EE / BC-AE\n- BC-EF / BC-AF\n- BC-EG / BC-AG\n- BC-EH / BC-AH\n\nwith attributes per entry. Then model_level characteristics matrix across these 9 group IDs.\n\nAlternatively 18 entries (16 unique + duplicated EB/AB as II variant = 18). That's heavy.\n\nI think per-column grouping is better because attributes are identical within a column. I'll use model_id like \"BC-EA/BC-AA\" and add an attribute-free label? The schema models entry: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I could put model_id \"BC-EA\" and label \"BC-EA / BC-AA\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me put each individual designation as its own model entry: model_id \"BC-EA\", attributes {} — no, then model_level matrix values need 18 rows per attribute, and the duplicate column problem persists (BC-EB would need two values).\n\nOK let me go with column groups (9 entries):\n1. model_id: \"BC-EA/BC-AA\"\n2. \"BC-EB/BC-AB\"\n3. \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\" — hmm, or \"(class II)\". The distinguishing feature: class II, Max 0.1–100 g. I'll use \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\".\n4. \"BC-EC/BC-AC\"\n5. \"BC-ED/BC-AD\"\n6. \"BC-EE/BC-AE\"\n7. \"BC-EF/BC-AF\"\n8. \"BC-EG/BC-AG\"\n9. \"BC-EH/BC-AH\"\n\nEach with attributes: accuracy_class, max, verification_scale_interval_e, digital_scale_interval_d, max_number_of_scale_intervals_n, tare_balancing_range, temperature_range_1, temperature_range_2 (where present), nominal_capacity_load_receptor, initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load, max_weighing_pan_size.\n\nWait — for table 4, the row says \"Temperature range\" (not \"Temperature range 1/2\"), with colspan 2: +10/+30 °C. So for EG/AG and EH/AH there's a single temperature range. And table 3 (EE/AE, EF/AF) has only \"Temperature range 1\" spanning both columns (+10/+30) and NO temperature range 2. Interesting.\n\nFor the model_level matrix in characteristics, I'll include the varying attributes: accuracy_class, max, e, d, n, nominal capacity, initial zero + dead load, pan size, temperature ranges. Tare-balancing range is uniform (until 100% of Max) → could be type_level, but it references Max. I'll put it in type_level as string \"until 100% of Max\" — hmm, per rules, value strings for formulas allowed. \"100% of Max\" → value: 100, unit: \"%\", reference \"Max\". I'll put type_level: tare_balancing_range: {value: 100, unit_symbol: \"%\"}? That loses \"of Max\". Maybe value: \"100% of Max\"? The rules say structured values strict, unit out of value. But \"100% of Max\" is like \"150% of Emax\" example → value 150, unit \"%\", plus separate reference characteristic. Rule example: `150% of Emax` → `value: 150, unit_symbol: \"%\"` and SEPARATE characteristic `safe_overload_reference: {value: \"Emax\"}`. So: tare_balancing_range: {value: 100, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, tare_balancing_reference: {value: \"Max\"}. OK.\n\nActually wait — is \"Tare-balancing range: until 100% of Max\" type_level? It spans all columns via colspan in all tables. Yes, uniform → type_level.\n\nNow let me carefully parse each column's values with unit extraction:\n\nTable 1 (page 2), columns C1, C2, C3:\n\nRow \"Type\": C1: \"BC-EA BC-AA\" → models BC-EA, BC-AA. C2: \"BC-EB BC-AB\". C3: \"BC-EB BC-AB\".\nRow \"Accuracy Class\": C1: (I), C2: (I), C3: (II).\nRow \"Max\": C1: \"50 g - 320 g\" → {min: 50, max: 320}, unit g. C2: \"50 g - 220 g\" → {50, 220} g. C3: \"0,1 g - 100 g\" → decimal comma: 0.1 → {0.1, 100} g.\nRow \"e\": C1: \"1 mg - 2 mg\" → {1, 2} mg. C2: \"1 mg - 2 mg\" → {1,2} mg. C3: \"1 mg - 2 mg\" → {1,2} mg.\nRow \"d\": C1: \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\" → {0.1, 2} mg. C2: same. C3: same.\nRow \"n\": C1: \"≤ 320 000\" → {min: null, max: 320000} (dimensionless). C2: ≤ 220000. C3: ≤ 100000.\nRow \"Tare-balancing range\": colspan 3: \"until 100% of Max\".\nRow \"Temperature range 1\": C1: \"+17 °C / +27 °C\" → {17, 27} °C. C2: same. C3: \"+10 °C / +30 °C\" → {10, 30} °C.\nRow \"Temperature range 2 (1)\": C1: {10,30} °C. C2: {10,30} °C. C3: {10,30} °C. Footnote 1).\nRow \"Nominal capacity of the load receptor\": C1: 384 g. C2: 264 g. C3: 264 g.\nRow \"Initial zero setting + dead load (2)\": C1: \"≤ 234 g\" → {min:null, max:234} g. C2: ≤ 214 g. C3: ≤ 263 g. Footnote 2).\nRow \"Maximum weighing pan size\": C1: \"Ø 90 mm\" → value \"Ø 90\"? It's diameter 90 mm. I'll record max_weighing_pan_size: {value: 90, unit_symbol: \"mm\"} with maybe diameter note. Could use value \"Ø 90 mm\"? Rule says extract unit out. Diameter isn't a unit; the pan size is Ø90mm. I'll do value: 90, unit_symbol: \"mm\", and label max_weighing_pan_diameter. For the rectangular ones \"180 mm x 180 mm\" → value: {min: 180, max: 180}? Better: value \"180 x 180\" with unit mm? Hmm. Rules: ranges like \"110-240\" become min/max. \"180 mm x 180 mm\" is a dimension pair. I'll use {min: 180, max: 180} unit mm for square pans, and for Ø use label with \"diameter\". Actually to be cleaner: for round pans attribute \"maximum_weighing_pan_diameter\" value 90 unit mm; for rectangular \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\" value {min:180,max:180} unit mm. Hmm, that introduces two attribute names. Alternatively keep \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\" with value string \"Ø 90 mm\"→ no, units must be separated.\n\nI'll do: attribute \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\": value: 90, unit_symbol: \"mm\" for C1-C3 (with the Ø implied...). Hmm, losing the shape info. Alternatively add \"maximum_weighing_pan_size_shape\": \"round\"/\"rectangular\". That's fabrication-ish but derived from \"Ø\". I think adding a shape attribute is reasonable extraction, not fabrication. But simpler: use value \"Ø 90\" with unit \"mm\"? \"Ø 90\" — value \"Ø 90\" as string, unit_symbol \"mm\". The value isn't numeric then. Rules prefer numbers. I'll go with {value: 90, unit_symbol: \"mm\"} and keep attribute name maximum_weighing_pan_size; for rectangular, {value: {min:180, max:180}, unit_symbol: \"mm\"}. Hmm, the min/max encoding for 180×180 is a bit off but acceptable. Actually, maybe better: value: {\"width\": 180, \"depth\": 180}? The schema allows value to be object {min,max} or string or number. A shape object deviates. I'll keep min/max.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe simpler to keep it as string \"180 mm x 180 mm\" → no, unit separation rule. I'll do {min: 180, max: 180}, unit mm. Fine.\n\nTable 2 (still page 2), columns D1 (BC-EC/BC-AC), D2 (BC-ED/BC-AD):\nAccuracy: (I), (II).\nMax: \"500 g – 1 500 g\" → {500, 1500} g; \"1 g - 650 g\" → {1, 650} g.\ne: \"10 mg - 20 mg\" → {10, 20} mg; \"0.01 g – 0.1 g\" → {0.01, 0.1} g. Hmm — units differ between columns (mg vs g). In the model_level matrix for e, each entry needs its own unit? The schema: model_level entries have attribute, unit, values[] per model. If units differ per model, I can split into two model_level entries: one for e in mg (models EA/AA, EB/AB, EB/AB-II, EC/AC) and one in g (ED/AD, EE/AE, EF/AF). Hmm: table 3: e \"0.1 g - 1 g\" for EE and EF; table 4: e \"1 g\" for EG, EH.\n\nAlternatively express all e in mg: {10,20} mg, {10,100} mg (0.01-0.1 g), {100,1000} mg, {1000} mg. Converting units — allowed? Rule says don't fabricate; unit conversion is arithmetic, generally safe. But maybe cleaner to keep original units and split matrix entries by unit. I'll split:\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- accuracy_class: values: I, I, II, I, II, II, II, II, II — value as string \"(I)\"? Canonical: \"I\" / \"II\". I'll use \"I\"/\"II\".\n- max: unit g — values with {min,max}.\n- verification_scale_interval_e: split by unit: mg for EA/AA, EB/AB, EB/AB(II), EC/AC; g for ED/AD, EE/AE, EF/AF, EG/AG, EH/AH. Actually EC/AC e is \"10 mg - 20 mg\" (mg), ED/AD \"0.01 g – 0.1 g\" (g), EE/AE and EF/AF \"0.1 g - 1 g\" (g), EG/EH \"1 g\" (g). So mg group: EA/AA {1,2}, EB/AB {1,2}, EB/AB(II) {1,2}, EC/AC {10,20}. g group: ED/AD {0.01,0.1}, EE/AE {0.1,1}, EF/AF {0.1,1}, EG/AG 1, EH/AH 1.\n- actual_scale_interval_d: mg group: EA/AA {0.1,2}, EB/AB {0.1,2}, EB/AB(II) {0.1,2}; EC/AC {1,20} mg (\"1 mg - 20 mg\"); ED/AD {0.001,0.1} g; EE/AE {0.01,1} g; EF/AF {0.01,1} g; EG/AG {0.1,1} g; EH/AH {0.1,1} g.\n- max_number_of_scale_intervals_n: ≤320000, ≤220000, ≤100000, ≤150000, ≤65000, ≤62000, ≤62000, ≤12200, ≤12200. Unit null (dimensionless).\n- temperature_range_1: {17,27} °C (EA/AA, EB/AB), {10,30} °C (EB/AB(II)), {17,27} (EC/AC), {10,30} (ED/AD), {10,30} (EE/AE, EF/AF), {10,30} (EG, EH — from \"Temperature range\" row; treat as temperature_range_1? The table 4 row label is just \"Temperature range\". Hmm — for consistency I could put it as temperature_range. I'll add a separate attribute \"temperature_range\" for EG/EH, or map to temperature_range_1. I think mapping to temperature_range_1 is reasonable since it serves the same role, but to be faithful I'll use \"temperature_range\" label for those two models. Hmm, that creates a sparse matrix entry with only 2 values. That's fine.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider: table 4 \"Temperature range\" +10/+30 spans both columns. Table 3 \"Temperature range 1\" +10/+30 spans both columns (no range 2). Table 1&2 have range 1 and range 2 (with footnote 1 on range 2). I'll emit:\n- temperature_range_1: values for EA/AA {17,27}, EB/AB {17,27}, EB/AB(II) {10,30}, EC/AC {17,27}, ED/AD {10,30}, EE/AE {10,30}, EF/AF {10,30}.\n- temperature_range_2: EA/AA {10,30} footnote 1, EB/AB {10,30} footnote 1, EB/AB(II) {10,30} footnote 1, EC/AC {10,30} fn1, ED/AD {10,30} fn1.\n- temperature_range: EG/AG {10,30}, EH/AH {10,30}.\n\n- nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor: 384, 264, 264, 1800, 780, 7440, 7440, 14640, 14640 g. (Note \"1 800 g\" → 1800; \"7 440 g\" → 7440; \"14 640 g\" → 14640.)\n- initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load: ≤234, ≤214, ≤263, ≤1300, ≤779, 6940 (no ≤ in table 3! \"6 940 g\" — written without ≤), ≤9640, ≤9640. Footnote 2. Note EE/AE and EF/AF show \"6 940 g\" without ≤ — extract as given: value 6940 (or {max: 6940}? No — no ≤ sign; I'll record 6940 as plain number). Hmm, \"Initial zero setting + dead load: 6 940 g\" without inequality. Faithful: 6940.\n- maximum_weighing_pan_size: C1-C3: 90 mm (Ø); EC/AC: 120 (Ø); ED/AD: 120 (Ø); EE/AE: 180×180; EF/AF: Ø 180; EG/AG: 180×180; EH/AH: Ø 180.\n\nShould pan size be model_level? Yes, varies (90 vs 120 vs 180). Include in matrix with unit mm.\n\nAlso include pan shape? I'll add attribute \"maximum_weighing_pan_shape\" with values \"round\"/\"square\"? Might be over-engineering. I'll include the diameter info via attribute name? Hmm. Let me just use maximum_weighing_pan_size with numeric value and a separate model_level attribute \"weighing_pan_shape\": values \"round (Ø)\" / \"rectangular\". Hmm... I think I'll include it — it preserves the \"Ø\" info. Values: EA/AA round, EB/AB round, EB/AB(II) round, EC/AC round, ED/AD round, EE/AE rectangular, EF/AF round, EG/AG rectangular, EH/AH round. And pan size numbers 90,90,90,120,120,180,180,180,180.\n\nAlternatively — simpler: keep maximum_weighing_pan_size with value like \"Ø 90\" ... no. OK going with shape attribute.\n\nHmm, actually, I worry about over-complexity. Let me decide the final structure now.\n\nAlso type_level characteristics:\n- tare_balancing_range: {value: 100, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — plus tare_balancing_reference: {value: \"Max\"}.\n- footnotes extracted:\n  1) \"Only for weighing instruments with incorporated span adjustment device being automatically released.\"\n  2) \"The sum of Max, initial zero setting range and dead load shall not exceed the nominal load of the load receptor.\"\n\nmatrix_tables: I could preserve the four annex tables as matrix_tables entries. The schema says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Since I'm populating model_family and model_level, the tables do fit elsewhere... but the layout is two-dimensional (attribute rows × model columns). I think including them in matrix_tables is good for fidelity: 4 tables with columns = attributes and rows = model groups. Let me include them: name \"Metrological characteristics\", columns: [\"model_group\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"e\", \"d\", \"n\", \"temperature_range_1\", \"temperature_range_2\", \"nominal_capacity_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\"], rows = the model groups. That's a transposed representation. Or keep original orientation: rows as attributes. The schema example has rows as model entries. I'll do rows as models.\n\nHmm, but is it redundant? The instruction: \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". These tables fit into model_level. But the 2-D original arrangement with per-table column grouping (which models share a table) is extra info. I'll include matrix_tables for fidelity — 4 entries. Actually, let me think about token budget and parseability. It's fine, include them.\n\nActually wait — I want to reconsider whether to represent the duplicate \"BC-EB BC-AB\" column. Let me look at the raw again:\n\n```\n<tr><td>Type</td><td align=\"center\">BC-EA<br>BC-AA</td><td align=\"center\">BC-EB<br>BC-AB</td><td align=\"center\">BC-EB<br>BC-AB</td></tr>\n```\n\nYes, duplicated. Given the actual METAS certificate (R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04)... let me think. The Sartorius BC-E line: models BCE/E-A? Actually, hmm, \"BC-EA to BC-EH\" — 8 models. Table1 col3 must be an error somewhere. Given the characteristics of col 3 (class II, Max 0.1-100 g, e 1-2 mg — class II with mg e is suspicious), I suspect the original PDF actually printed \"BC-EB BC-AB\" there too (a typo in the source) OR OCR misread. I can't verify. Faithful extraction: keep as \"BC-EB/BC-AB\" with a distinguishing suffix, and note it. I'll add a note in the matrix_table description mentioning the duplicate designation.\n\nAlternatively — treat col 3 as the class II variant of EB/AB: model_id \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\".\n\nAlso accuracy_class values with parenthesized notation \"( I )\". Canonical \"I\" or \"II\".\n\nNow recommendation: R76. id \"R76\" (canonical, e.g. \"R60\"). edition: the cert cites \"OIML R 76-1, edition 2006\". edition as int: 2006 (edition year). amendment: null. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: [\"I\", \"II\"] (from \"( I ), ( II )\").\n\nCertificate number: \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\". Should I include \"Rev. 3\"? There's no revision field in certificate schema; revision_history captures revisions. But the document number on annexes is \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\". I'll set number to \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\" and maybe note revision 3... The schema has no field. I could include number as \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04 Rev. 3\"? Rule says don't fabricate; the header shows \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04 Rev. 3\". Hmm. I'll use \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\" and rely on revision_history. Actually, adding the revision info somewhere useful: I could add \"revision\": \"3\"? Not in schema; but schema says produce JSON following the layered schema — extra fields maybe tolerated? Better to stick to schema. The revision_history includes rev 03 with date 2021-04-15, which matches date_issued. Good.\n\nproject_number: not present — omit. page_total: 4.\n\ndate_issued: \"2021-04-15\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"CH1\".\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"6030-01225\", date: \"2019-09-24\", pages: 13, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"6030-01225-01\", date: \"2020-09-21\", pages: 19, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"6030-01225-02\", date: \"2020-11-03\", pages: 19, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"6030-01225-03\", date: \"2020-04-15\", pages: 19, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — note: date \"2020-04-15\" looks like a possible typo in the cert (should be 2021?) but extract as given.\n\ndocumentation file: test_reports[] with role \"documentation_file\": {id: \"R076_2006-A-CH1-2019.04_Rev_03_LERD\", date: \"2021-04-15\", pages: 7, role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"00\", date: \"2019-09-24\", changes: \"First issue\"}\n- {revision: \"01\", date: \"2020-09-21\", changes: \"Extension to the types BC-EA to BC-EH, BC-AA to BC-AH\"}\n- {revision: \"02\", date: \"2020-11-03\", changes: \"Editorial changes\"}\n- {revision: \"03\", date: \"2021-04-15\", changes: \"Rebranding, new design\"}\n\nfootnotes:\n- {marker: \"1)\", text: \"Only for weighing instruments with incorporated span adjustment device being automatically released.\"}\n- {marker: \"2)\", text: \"The sum of Max, initial zero setting range and dead load shall not exceed the nominal load of the load receptor.\"}\n\ncomponents: none — omit.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\"\n- type_designations: list all 16: [\"BC-EA\",\"BC-EB\",\"BC-EC\",\"BC-ED\",\"BC-EE\",\"BC-EF\",\"BC-EG\",\"BC-EH\",\"BC-AA\",\"BC-AB\",\"BC-AC\",\"BC-AD\",\"BC-AE\",\"BC-AF\",\"BC-AG\",\"BC-AH\"]\n- module_designation: null (omit? schema shows \"Not applicable\" | null). I'll put null. Actually should I include with null or omit? Rule 8: omit if not present. The certificate has a \"Module\" field with content \"BC-EA to BC-EH, BC-AA to BC-AH\" — but that's the model range, not a module per OIML modular approval sense. Since schema module_designation expects \"Analog load cell\"|... , setting \"Not applicable\" seems right for a balance certificate. I'll set \"Not applicable\". Hmm, or omit. I'll include null... Let me include \"Not applicable\" — because the instrument is a complete balance, no modules. Actually, safer: null (unknown). Hmm. The cert doesn't discuss modules. I'll omit the field entirely? The schema lists it under certified_type; omission allowed. I'll set it to null to be explicit. Eh — I'll just omit it to follow rule 8. Hmm, but \"Not applicable\" is a valid enum. The type is a complete weighing instrument; but I can't be 100% sure the cert doesn't mention modules — it doesn't. I'll go with null.\n\nActually, let me include \"module_designation\": null.\n\n- description: \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"BC-E and BC-A Line\" — or \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". I'll use \"BC-E and BC-A Line\".\n- models: I'll do 9 group entries? Or 18 individual? Let me decide: individual model entries are more aligned with \"one entry per variant\". But attributes are shared per column, and the E/A pairing is how the cert presents it. Doing 18 entries duplicates data ×2 in models AND ×2 in model_level values (18 values per attribute). That's verbose but more granular. The duplicate column makes 18 → but BC-EB and BC-AB each have two spec sets — model_id must be unique.\n\nI'll go with column groups (9 entries), model_id like \"BC-EA/BC-AA\". Add attributes to each model entry? The schema allows attributes{} in model entries; but model_level matrix already covers attributes. Redundant. The R137 example shows models entries without attributes (attributes live in model_level). I'll keep models entries minimal: model_id (+ maybe label).\n\nWait, but the R60 example shows models with just model_id. OK.\n\nFor the duplicate: model_id \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\". Hmm, alternatively \"BC-EB/BC-AB variant 2\". I'll add label fields: for entry 2, label \"Accuracy class I, Max 50 g – 220 g\"; entry 3 label \"Accuracy class II, Max 0.1 g – 100 g\". That disambiguates. Let me set labels like: entry1 model_id \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", label null? I'll give labels describing class: e.g. \"class I\". Hmm keep simple: label: \"accuracy class I\" etc.\n\nmodel_level characteristics — list:\n\n1. accuracy_class (unit null): values:\n- BC-EA/BC-AA: \"I\"\n- BC-EB/BC-AB: \"I\"\n- BC-EB/BC-AB (class II): \"II\"\n- BC-EC/BC-AC: \"I\"\n- BC-ED/BC-AD: \"II\"\n- BC-EE/BC-AE: \"II\"\n- BC-EF/BC-AF: \"II\"\n- BC-EG/BC-AG: \"II\"\n- BC-EH/BC-AH: \"II\"\n\n2. maximum_capacity (Max): unit \"g\":\n- EA/AA {50,320}; EB/AB {50,220}; EB/AB-II {0.1,100}; EC/AC {500,1500}; ED/AD {1,650}; EE/AE {500,6200}; EF/AF {500,6200}; EG/AG {5000,12200}; EH/AH {5000,12200}.\n\n3. verification_scale_interval_e: split by unit:\n   a) unit \"mg\": EA/AA {1,2}; EB/AB {1,2}; EB/AB-II {1,2}; EC/AC {10,20}.\n   b) unit \"g\": ED/AD {0.01,0.1}; EE/AE {0.1,1}; EF/AF {0.1,1}; EG/AG 1; EH/AH 1.\n\n4. actual_scale_interval_d:\n   a) unit \"mg\": EA/AA {0.1,2}; EB/AB {0.1,2}; EB/AB-II {0.1,2}; EC/AC {1,20}.\n   b) unit \"g\": ED/AD {0.001,0.1}; EE/AE {0.01,1}; EF/AF {0.01,1}; EG/AG {0.1,1}; EH/AH {0.1,1}.\n\n5. max_number_of_scale_intervals (n): unit null: max 320000; 220000; 100000; 150000; 65000; 62000; 62000; 12200; 12200. Values as {min:null, max:X}.\n\n6. temperature_range_1: unit °C: EA/AA {17,27}; EB/AB {17,27}; EB/AB-II {10,30}; EC/AC {17,27}; ED/AD {10,30}; EE/AE {10,30}; EF/AF {10,30}.\n\n7. temperature_range_2: unit °C, footnote \"1)\": EA/AA {10,30}; EB/AB {10,30}; EB/AB-II {10,30}; EC/AC {10,30}; ED/AD {10,30}.\n\n8. temperature_range: unit °C: EG/AG {10,30}; EH/AH {10,30}. (from table 4 \"Temperature range\")\n\n9. nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor: unit g: 384; 264; 264; 1800; 780; 7440; 7440; 14640; 14640.\n\n10. initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load: unit g, footnote \"2)\": {max:234}; {max:214}; {max:263}; {max:1300}; {max:779}; 6940 (plain); 6940; {max:9640}; {max:9640}.\n\nHmm wait — EE/AE row: \"6 940 g\" (no ≤). Others have \"≤\". I'll keep faithful: 6940 as number for EE/AE and EF/AF; the others as {min:null,max:X}. Should I keep the whole entry uniform? Faithfulness wins; keep as-is.\n\n11. maximum_weighing_pan_size: unit mm: 90; 90; 90; 120; 120; {180,180}; 180 (Ø for EF? EF is Ø 180 → 180); {180,180}; 180. Plus shape attribute:\n\n12. weighing_pan_shape: \"round\"/\"rectangular\": round, round, round, round, round, rectangular, round, rectangular, round.\n\nHmm, maybe name it \"maximum_weighing_pan_shape\". Values as strings without unit. I'll include.\n\ntype_level characteristics:\n- tare_balancing_range: {value: 100, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — plus tare_balancing_reference: {value: \"Max\"}.\n\nActually per the rule-3 example, \"150% of Emax\" → value 150, unit \"%\" + safe_overload_reference {value: \"Emax\"}. So:\n- tare_balancing_range: value 100, unit \"%\", unit_id null.\n- tare_balancing_range_reference: value \"Max\".\n\nAnything else type-level? The temperature limits etc. are model-dependent. n is model dependent. Fine.\n\nmatrix_tables: 4 entries preserving original tables.\n\nTable A (page 2, first):\nname: \"Metrological characteristics — BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB (I), BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\"\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"verification_scale_interval_e\", \"actual_scale_interval_d\", \"max_number_of_scale_intervals\", \"tare_balancing_range\", \"temperature_range_1\", \"temperature_range_2\", \"nominal_capacity_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"g\", null(mixed), null(mixed), null, \"%\", \"°C\", \"°C\", \"g\", \"g\", \"mm\"] — hmm units for e/d vary. The schema example column_units exists. I'll give per-column dominant unit or null where mixed. For e/d I'll put null and keep values with units embedded? No wait — in matrix rows, values must follow structured form? The schema example matrix_tables rows have plain numbers ({\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...}). So matrix table rows use plain values, units in column_units. For mixed-unit columns (e, d), values like \"1 mg - 2 mg\" would embed units as strings. Acceptable for the preservation table? The R137 example used numbers with column units. For mixed, I'll use strings with units. Hmm, but rule 2 strictness is about characteristics values... For matrix_tables, the example shows raw values. I'll keep units embedded in strings for e/d/max columns where they're uniform: max column has g throughout → put \"g\" in column_units and values {min,max}. e/d mixed → strings \"1 mg - 2 mg\" / \"0.01 g – 0.1 g\"? That mixes. Alternatively convert e/d to mg uniformly: e: 1–2 mg, 1–2, 1–2, 10–20, 10–100, 100–1000, 100–1000, 1000, 1000 mg; d: 0.1–2, 0.1–2, 0.1–2, 1–20, 1–100, 10–1000, 10–1000, 100–1000, 100–1000 mg. That's clean and faithful (unit conversions). Then column_units for e and d = \"mg\".\n\nHmm, conversions: 0.01 g = 10 mg ✓; 0.1 g = 100 mg ✓; 1 g = 1000 mg ✓; 0.001 g = 1 mg ✓. Good.\n\nBut for model_level characteristics, I earlier decided to split by original units. Alternatively also use mg everywhere in model_level for e and d. Consistency: use mg everywhere. But then table 2's \"0.01 g – 0.1 g\" becomes {10,100} mg — a derived conversion. It's accurate. I think uniform mg is cleaner and less error-prone for consumers. However, faithfulness to source units... The rule 2 says unit separation; doesn't forbid conversion. The R137 example kept source units. Mixed-unit columns are awkward. Let me keep original units and split model_level entries by unit (two entries for e, two for d). That preserves source data without conversion. For matrix_tables, columns with mixed units → use strings with units embedded, column_units null for those columns. E.g. \"1 mg - 2 mg\" and \"0.01 g – 0.1 g\". Fine.\n\nActually hold on — for max column: table 1 col 3 Max \"0,1 g - 100 g\" → 0.1–100 g; all in g ✓.\n\nLet me now also double check e for EB/AB-II: \"1 mg - 2 mg\" ✓. And d \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\" ✓. n ≤ 100 000 ✓.\n\nInitial zero for C3: \"≤ 263 g\" ✓.\n\nNow, should matrix_tables include all four tables as separate entries? Yes:\n1. \"Metrological characteristics (page 2, table 1)\" — models BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB (I), BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\n2. \"Metrological characteristics (page 2, table 2)\" — BC-EC/BC-AC, BC-ED/BC-AD\n3. \"Metrological characteristics (page 3, table 1)\" — BC-EE/BC-AE, BC-EF/BC-AF\n4. \"Metrological characteristics (page 3, table 2)\" — BC-EG/BC-AG, BC-EH/BC-AH\n\nFootnotes per table: 1) on temperature range 2; 2) on initial zero setting. I'll attach footnote markers in the values (as strings like \"≤ 234 g 2)\"? no — matrix rows plain). I'll include footnotes array per table with the texts. Simpler: include table-level footnotes [\"1) Only for...\", \"2) The sum of...\"] for tables 1-2; for table 3 (EE/EF): footnote 2 only; table 4: footnote 2 only.\n\nHmm, wait: table 3's initial zero row has sup 2) — yes. Table 4 has sup 2) too. Footnote 1 appears in tables 1&2 (temperature range 2). Table 3 has only \"Temperature range 1\" so no footnote 1. Table 4 \"Temperature range\" no footnote.\n\nFor matrix rows, do I include footnote markers? The example rows don't. I'll keep plain and use the footnotes array.\n\nAlso tare_balancing_range column: \"until 100% of Max\" string in rows, column_units null.\n\nPan size column: values \"Ø 90 mm\" / \"Ø 120 mm\" / \"180 mm x 180 mm\" / \"Ø 180 mm\" — keep as strings with unit embedded, column_units null? Or unit \"mm\" and values \"Ø 90\", \"180 x 180\". I'll embed as raw strings: \"Ø 90 mm\" etc., column_units null. Hmm, but structured-ness... matrix_tables is the preservation area, raw strings OK.\n\nTemperature columns: \"+17 °C / +27 °C\" strings or {17,27}? I'll use raw strings for preservation fidelity: \"+17 °C / +27 °C\". column_units null. Hmm, the R137 example used numbers with column_units... mixed approaches across tables is OK I guess. But let me be a bit more structured where units are uniform:\n\nTable rows format decision — I'll use:\n- max: {min,max} with column unit \"g\"\n- e: string \"1 mg - 2 mg\" (column_units null)\n- d: string\n- n: number or string \"≤ 320 000\"? I'll use number with column_units null and column name \"max_number_of_scale_intervals_n\"; but ≤ lost. Use string \"≤ 320 000\"? I'll keep string to preserve ≤. Hmm. Let me just preserve raw strings for everything except simple numbers? The point of matrix_tables is preservation; model_level carries structured values. So raw strings in matrix_tables are fine and safest.\n\nDecision: matrix_tables rows contain raw-ish strings as in source, with column_units null, and a description. This avoids inconsistency.\n\nActually hmm, let me reconsider: is including 4 matrix tables + full model_level + models redundant bloat? It's allowed and useful. I'll include but keep them compact.\n\nNow, the description for table 1 should note the duplicate type designation. In description: \"Third column repeats the designation BC-EB/BC-AB with class II characteristics (as printed in the certificate).\"\n\nNow models list — final:\n\n```json\n\"model_family\": {\n  \"family_name\": \"BC-E and BC-A Line\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", \"label\": \"Accuracy class I\", \"attributes\": {}},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nShould attributes{} be empty or hold the specs? To avoid duplication with model_level, keep {} (or omit). Schema shows attributes{} in models entry; R60/R137 examples omit it. I'll omit attributes.\n\nLabels: give each a short label: \"class I, Max 50–320 g\" etc. Nice for disambiguation of the EB duplicate. Let me craft:\n- BC-EA/BC-AA: \"Class I\"\n- BC-EB/BC-AB: \"Class I\"\n- BC-EB/BC-AB (class II): \"Class II variant, Max 0.1–100 g\" — hmm as printed the designation is identical; my label clarifies. OK.\n\nShould model_id use \"/\" or \" + \"? I'll use \"BC-EA/BC-AA\". In model_level values, \"model\" field must match model_id.\n\nNow the 9 group model_ids:\n1. \"BC-EA/BC-AA\"\n2. \"BC-EB/BC-AB\"\n3. \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\"\n4. \"BC-EC/BC-AC\"\n5. \"BC-ED/BC-AD\"\n6. \"BC-EE/BC-AE\"\n7. \"BC-EF/BC-AF\"\n8. \"BC-EG/BC-AG\"\n9. \"BC-EH/BC-AH\"\n\nmodel_level entries (final list):\n\n1. accuracy_class:\nvalues: [{model: \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", value: \"I\"}, {model: \"BC-EB/BC-AB\", value: \"I\"}, {model: \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\", value: \"II\"}, {model: \"BC-EC/BC-AC\", value: \"I\"}, {model: \"BC-ED/BC-AD\", value: \"II\"}, {model: \"BC-EE/BC-AE\", value: \"II\"}, {model: \"BC-EF/BC-AF\", value: \"II\"}, {model: \"BC-EG/BC-AG\", value: \"II\"}, {model: \"BC-EH/BC-AH\", value: \"II\"}]\n\nWait — accuracy class values: should they be strings \"I\"/\"II\"? Yes.\n\nHmm, but the rule 2 says every characteristic value must be an object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. For model_level, the structure given in the prompt examples is {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — no unit per value; unit at attribute level. Footnote markers per value shown in schema? The R60 example: {\"model\": \"Emax=25t\", \"value\": 25}. The schema line: values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. So values are {model, value, footnote_markers}. Units at the matrix entry level. But what about structured objects (min/max)? In my model_level, value can be {min,max} object. OK.\n\nFor accuracy_class values — strings \"I\"/\"II\". unit null.\n\n2. maximum_capacity (Max):\nattribute: \"maximum_capacity\", unit_symbol \"g\":\n- EA/AA {50,320}\n- EB/AB {50,220}\n- EB-II {0.1,100}\n- EC/AC {500,1500}\n- ED/AD {1,650}\n- EE/AE {500,6200}\n- EF/AF {500,6200}\n- EG/AG {5000,12200}\n- EH/AH {5000,12200}\n\n3. verification_scale_interval_e, mg:\n- EA/AA {1,2}; EB/AB {1,2}; EB-II {1,2}; EC/AC {10,20}\n\n4. verification_scale_interval_e, g:\n- ED/AD {0.01,0.1}; EE/AE {0.1,1}; EF/AF {0.1,1}; EG/AG 1; EH/AH 1\n\n5. actual_scale_interval_d, mg:\n- EA/AA {0.1,2}; EB/AB {0.1,2}; EB-II {0.1,2}; EC/AC {1,20}\n\n6. actual_scale_interval_d, g:\n- ED/AD {0.001,0.1}; EE/AE {0.01,1}; EF/AF {0.01,1}; EG/AG {0.1,1}; EH/AH {0.1,1}\n\n7. max_number_of_scale_intervals_nlc? No — this is a balance; n is the number of verification scale intervals. Attribute: \"max_number_of_scale_intervals\". unit null.\n- EA/AA {min:null,max:320000}; EB/AB max 220000; EB-II max 100000; EC/AC max 150000; ED/AD max 65000; EE/AE max 62000; EF/AF max 62000; EG/AG max 12200; EH/AH max 12200.\n\n8. temperature_range_1 (°C):\n- EA/AA {17,27}; EB/AB {17,27}; EB-II {10,30}; EC/AC {17,27}; ED/AD {10,30}; EE/AE {10,30}; EF/AF {10,30}\n\n9. temperature_range_2 (°C, footnote \"1)\"):\n- EA/AA {10,30}; EB/AB {10,30}; EB-II {10,30}; EC/AC {10,30}; ED/AD {10,30}\nEach value: {\"model\": ..., \"value\": {min:10,max:30}, \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]}\n\n10. temperature_range (°C):\n- EG/AG {10,30}; EH/AH {10,30}\n\n11. nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor (g):\n- 384; 264; 264; 1800; 780; 7440; 7440; 14640; 14640\n\n12. initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load (g, footnote \"2)\"):\n- EA/AA {max:234}; EB/AB {max:214}; EB-II {max:263}; EC/AC {max:1300}; ED/AD {max:779}; EE/AE 6940; EF/AF 6940; EG/AG {max:9640}; EH/AH {max:9640}\n\n13. maximum_weighing_pan_size (mm):\n- EA/AA 90; EB/AB 90; EB-II 90; EC/AC 120; ED/AD 120; EE/AE {180,180}; EF/AF 180; EG/AG {180,180}; EH/AH 180\n\nHmm — EE/AE \"180 mm x 180 mm\" → {min:180,max:180}; EF \"Ø 180 mm\" → 180.\n\n14. weighing_pan_shape:\n- round; round; round; round; round; rectangular; round; rectangular; round\n\nShould shape be in model_level or in models[].attributes? Putting in models.attributes could be neat but I chose minimal models. model_level with value strings works.\n\ntype_level:\n- tare_balancing_range: {value: 100, unit_symbol: \"%\", unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- tare_balancing_range_reference: {value: \"Max\"}\n\nHmm also, should I include something like \"n\" definition? No.\n\nDo I include \"accuracy_classes\" at type level? That's in recommendation.\n\nrecommendation:\n{ \"id\": \"R76\", \"edition\": 2006, \"amendment\": null, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"I\", \"II\"] }\n\nNote the cited document is \"R 76-1\" — part 1. id canonical \"R76\". The cert text: \"OIML R 76-1, edition 2006\". Should I record id \"R76\"? Rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". I'll use \"R76\" and maybe note part... no field for part. Fine.\n\ncertificate:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": null/omit,\n  \"page_total\": 4,\n  \"member_state\": \"Switzerland\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2021-04-15\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"CH1\"\n}\nOmit project_number (not present).\n\nissuing_authority:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Federal Institute of Metrology METAS\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Lindenweg 50\", \"3003 Bern-Wabern\", \"Switzerland\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"Gulian Couvreur\",\n  \"person_title\": \"Head of METAS-Cert\",\n  \"phone\": \"+41 58 387 01 11\",\n  \"website\": \"www.metas.ch\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"CH1\"\n}\nAlso \"Conformity Evaluation Body METAS-Cert\" — part of name block. I could add to name? name: \"Federal Institute of Metrology METAS\"; maybe address or a second line? I'll keep name as METAS and mention METAS-Cert in person_title. Fine.\n\napplicants[0]:\n{\"name\": \"Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG\", \"address_lines\": [\"Otto-Brenner-Strasse 20\", \"37079 Göttingen\", \"Germany\"]}\n\nmanufacturers[0]:\n{\"name\": \"Sartorius Scientific Instruments (Beijing) Co, Limited\", \"address_lines\": [\"Yu An Road No. 33, Zone B\", \"Tianzhu Airport Industrialzone, Shunyi District\", \"101 300 Beijing\", \"PR China\"]}\n\ntest_reports: 4 type evaluation reports + 1 documentation file. Wait — role enum: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. The cert says \"OIML type evaluation reports\" for the four, documentation file for LERD. So:\n\n[\n {\"id\": \"6030-01225\", \"date\": \"2019-09-24\", \"pages\": 13, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"6030-01225-01\", \"date\": \"2020-09-21\", \"pages\": 19, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"6030-01225-02\", \"date\": \"2020-11-03\", \"pages\": 19, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"6030-01225-03\", \"date\": \"2020-04-15\", \"pages\": 19, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n {\"id\": \"R076_2006-A-CH1-2019.04_Rev_03_LERD\", \"date\": \"2021-04-15\", \"pages\": 7, \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n\n\"Including pages\" — is it page count or page numbers? \"Including pages: 13\" likely means number of pages included (13 pages). I'll use pages: 13.\n\nrevision_history as above.\n\nfootnotes as above.\n\nNow matrix_tables — let me write them out.\n\nTable 1:\n{\n \"name\": \"Metrological characteristics — BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB, BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\",\n \"description\": \"Third column is printed with the same type designations BC-EB/BC-AB but with accuracy class II and Max 0.1 g – 100 g; reproduced as in the certificate.\",\n \"columns\": [\"model\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"verification_scale_interval_e\", \"actual_scale_interval_d\", \"max_number_of_scale_intervals_n\", \"temperature_range_1\", \"temperature_range_2\", \"nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\"],\n \"column_units\": [null, null, \"g\", null, null, null, \"°C\", \"°C\", \"g\", \"g\", \"mm\"],\n \"rows\": [\n   {\"model\": \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", \"accuracy_class\": \"I\", \"max\": \"50 g - 320 g\", ...}\n ]\n}\n\nHmm, I earlier said raw strings for preservation. Let me finalize row values:\n\nRow 1: model \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", accuracy_class \"( I )\"? Use \"I\". max \"50 g - 320 g\", e \"1 mg - 2 mg\", d \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\", n \"≤ 320 000\", temp1 \"+17 °C / +27 °C\", temp2 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", nom cap 384, init zero \"≤ 234 g\", pan \"Ø 90 mm\".\n\nIf column_units says g for max, then max value should be numeric {min,max} or \"50 - 320\". Ugh. To keep simple and consistent: I'll NOT use column_units at all and embed units in all row values as raw strings (true preservation). The schema lists columns[] and rows[]; column_units optional (shown in example but not required). I'll include column_units: null-entries? If I embed units, omit column_units entirely or set all null. I'll omit column_units and use raw strings. Hmm, but the example includes column_units... it's fine, optional.\n\nActually — let me reconsider once more. Two competing goals: preservation fidelity vs structured. I already have fully structured model_level. matrix_tables = preservation. Raw strings it is.\n\nRows table 1:\n- {\"model\": \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", \"accuracy_class\": \"I\", \"max\": \"50 g - 320 g\", \"e\": \"1 mg - 2 mg\", \"d\": \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\", \"n\": \"≤ 320 000\", \"temperature_range_1\": \"+17 °C / +27 °C\", \"temperature_range_2\": \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", \"nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor\": \"384 g\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\": \"≤ 234 g\", \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\": \"Ø 90 mm\"}\n- {\"model\": \"BC-EB/BC-AB\", ..., \"max\": \"50 g - 220 g\", n \"≤ 220 000\", cap \"264 g\", init \"≤ 214 g\", pan \"Ø 90 mm\"}\n- {\"model\": \"BC-EB/BC-AB (class II)\", accuracy_class \"II\", \"max\": \"0.1 g - 100 g\", e \"1 mg - 2 mg\", d \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\", n \"≤ 100 000\", temp1 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", temp2 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", cap \"264 g\", init \"≤ 263 g\", pan \"Ø 90 mm\"}\n\nNote: Max \"0,1 g\" — decimal comma in source. In raw preservation, keep \"0.1 g\"? Rule says convert European decimals to point. I'll write \"0.1 g - 100 g\". Good.\n\nfootnotes for table 1: the two footnote texts.\n\nTable 2:\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"e\", \"d\", \"n\", \"temperature_range_1\", \"temperature_range_2\", \"nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\"]\nrows:\n- BC-EC/BC-AC: I, \"500 g – 1 500 g\"→\"500 g - 1500 g\", e \"10 mg - 20 mg\", d \"1 mg - 20 mg\", n \"≤ 150 000\", t1 \"+17 °C / +27 °C\", t2 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", cap \"1 800 g\" → \"1800 g\"? Raw preservation vs number formatting: \"1 800 g\" has space thousands separator. I'll write \"1800 g\" to be safe/consistent. init \"≤ 1300 g\", pan \"Ø 120 mm\".\n- BC-ED/BC-AD: II, \"1 g - 650 g\", e \"0.01 g - 0.1 g\" (source \"0.01 g – 0.1 g\"), d \"0.001 g - 0.1 g\", n \"≤ 65 000\"→\"≤ 65000\", t1 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", t2 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", cap \"780 g\", init \"≤ 779 g\", pan \"Ø 120 mm\".\n\nTable 3:\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"e\", \"d\", \"n\", \"temperature_range_1\", \"nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\"]\nrows:\n- BC-EE/BC-AE: II, \"500 g - 6200 g\", e \"0.1 g - 1 g\", d \"0.01 g - 1 g\", n \"≤ 62000\", t1 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", cap \"7440 g\", init \"6940 g\", pan \"180 mm x 180 mm\"\n- BC-EF/BC-AF: II, \"500 g - 6200 g\", e \"0.1 g - 1 g\", d \"0.01 g - 1 g\", n \"≤ 62000\", t1 \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", cap \"7440 g\", init \"6940 g\", pan \"Ø 180 mm\"\nfootnotes: [2)]\n\nTable 4:\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"e\", \"d\", \"n\", \"temperature_range\", \"nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\"]\nrows:\n- BC-EG/BC-AG: II, \"5000 g - 12200 g\", e \"1 g\", d \"0.1 g - 1 g\", n \"≤ 12200\", temp \"+10 °C / +30 °C\", cap \"14640 g\", init \"≤ 9640 g\", pan \"180 mm x 180 mm\"\n- BC-EH/BC-AH: II, same, pan \"Ø 180 mm\"\nfootnotes: [2)]\n\nNames: \"Metrological characteristics (table 1 of 4)\" etc.\n\nNow, footnote markers on model_level values: temperature_range_2 values get [\"1)\"], initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load values get [\"2)\"].\n\nThe tare balancing: type_level with value 100, unit \"%\". And reference.\n\nLet me also double-check the Max row of table 3: \"500 g - 6 200 g\" → 500–6200 ✓. Table 4: \"5 000 g - 12 200 g\" → 5000–12200 ✓.\n\nn for EE/EF: \"≤ 62 000\" → 62000 ✓. EG/EH: \"≤ 12 200\" → 12200 ✓.\n\nNominal capacity EG/EH: \"14 640 g\" → 14640 ✓. Initial \"≤ 9 640 g\" → 9640 ✓.\n\nEC/AC cap \"1 800 g\" → 1800 ✓. Init \"≤ 1300 g\" ✓.\n\nNow certified_type.description: \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". Also category: R76 → \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Hmm — schema category examples list instrument categories; balances under R76 are NAWIs. I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n\ntype_designations: include the \"Line\" names too? The Type field: \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". Designations: BC-E and BC-A are line names; specific types BC-EA..EH, BC-AA..AH. I'll list the 16 model designations. Maybe also include \"BC-E\" and \"BC-A\"? They're family names, not type designations. Keep 16.\n\nNow — scheme A. certificate.scheme \"A\".\n\nAlso there's \"OIML-CS CERTIFICATE ISSUED UNDER SCHEME A\" ✓.\n\nPerson title: issuing authority table says \"Gulian Couvreur, Head of METAS-Cert\". Later approved by: \"Gulian Couvreur, Head of sector, METAS-Cert\". Use \"Head of METAS-Cert\".\n\nWebsite: \"www.metas.ch\". Email: none. Phone: \"+41 58 387 01 11\".\n\nAddress lines issuing authority: [\"Lindenweg 50\", \"3003 Bern-Wabern\", \"Switzerland\"].\n\nNow assemble JSON. Check parseability: no LaTeX, no stray characters. Use \"°C\" fine. \"≤\" fine in JSON strings.\n\nOne more consideration — model_level attribute names:\n- \"maximum_capacity\" (per R60 example label maximum_capacity). Good.\n- e: \"verification_scale_interval\" (canonical name in OIML: e = verification scale interval). Label \"verification_scale_interval\".\n- d: \"actual_scale_interval\".\n- n: \"max_number_of_verification_scale_intervals\"? I'll use \"max_number_of_scale_intervals\".\n- temperature_range_1, temperature_range_2, temperature_range.\n- \"nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor\".\n- \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\".\n- \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\".\n- \"maximum_weighing_pan_shape\"? I'll name \"weighing_pan_shape\" — hmm keep consistent: \"maximum_weighing_pan_shape\". Eh, \"weighing_pan_shape\" simpler. Let me use \"weighing_pan_shape\".\n\nValues for shape: \"round\" / \"rectangular\". unit null.\n\nAlso should accuracy_class attribute be at model_level — yes.\n\nUnit for n: null. Value {min:null, max:320000}. The structured form requires value, unit_symbol, unit_id? In model_level values, the schema example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — no unit_symbol per value. Units at matrix level. So values entries: {model, value, footnote_markers?}. For dimensionless n: attribute entry unit_symbol: null.\n\nBut hold on — rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. But the matrix examples override with flat values. The model_level structure in the schema: values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. So within model_level, values are flat with unit at attribute level. I'll follow the schema/examples: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [...]}.\n\nFor type_level, values are the full structured objects: {\"value\": 100, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}.\n\nLet me also decide whether to include type_level at all given only tare balancing. Yes, include.\n\nFull JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 4,\n    \"member_state\": \"Switzerland\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2021-04-15\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"CH1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Federal Institute of Metrology METAS\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Lindenweg 50\", \"3003 Bern-Wabern\", \"Switzerland\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Gulian Couvreur\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Head of METAS-Cert\",\n    \"phone\": \"+41 58 387 01 11\",\n    \"website\": \"www.metas.ch\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"CH1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Sarto